  "finding": "The existing borrowed sync API has a real packet copy for general callables, but simply making it canonical reaches many shared asynchronous core helpers and array-typed delegates. The second compiler pass reports82 body/consumer mismatches after the three initial signature/forwarder corrections.",
  "contract": "Shared async helpers really retain inputs through callbacks, constructors and cache/descriptor work. New per-call copies/clearing wrappers around those helpers would relocate rather than remove the packaging phase. A coherent future migration must consume arguments into the actual prepared state before suspension, together with its owned storage.",
  "scope": "No call/frame arena, native proof, compatibility switch or delegate dispatch framework introduced. Do not create another owned-callable adapter hierarchy merely to make the signature migration compile.",
  "limitations": "No working candidate or timing was produced, so no performance/semantic rejection follows from compiler failures. Original source, partial candidate, all build failures and correction are preserved. This is a coupled preparation/ownership scope finding, not a one-attempt or permanent design veto.",
  "next": "Use a representative affected construction/callback workload to establish actual retained-packet/preparation cost before selecting the coupled redesign. Current text call-entry4.29% alone cannot justify a repo-wide rewrite. Inspect the existing constructor CSV case; no new benchmark framework or broad ownership audit.",
